 "reading": {
  "split_loses": "pair c=2: MTP output share 1.8% -> 69.3%, decode 17.08 -> 14.17 tok/s end to end and 21.33 -> 17.51 tok/s excluding TTFT. pair c=4: share 2.1% -> 73.9%, 18.63 -> 14.65 end to end and 24.68 -> 16.94 excluding TTFT. long2 c=4: share 0.7% -> 11.1%, 16.75 -> 15.86 end to end and 23.02 -> 19.26 excluding TTFT. long2 c=2 is the one flat point (16.82 -> 16.70, share 1.1% -> 3.4%) because both of its rows cross the 1023-token window after 19 tokens, so the split has almost no window to act in.",
  "acceptance_was_not_the_limit": "in pair c=2 the healthy row accepted depth 3 on all 162 of its cycles (318 accepted draft tokens, 2.96 emitted tokens per cycle, 99.8% MTP coverage) and still cost 111.1 ms per token against 92.1 ms for the same row when the group stayed autoregressive, and 91.8 ms for the pure-AR control. At c=4 the same row cost 117.6 ms against 92.7 ms. Coverage and acceptance are not what the split pays for.",
  "why": "one one-row provider cycle and the co-resident row's autoregressive step serialize inside the same tick, so the split replaces one batched AR decode per tick with N provider cycles plus N AR steps. The cycle's own cost (provider step, K+1-row target verify, commit) is not amortized the way a batched AR step is, so the per-token cost rises even when every cycle returns the full depth-3 acceptance.",
  "route_cost_is_separate": "both speculative arms sit below their AR controls on these long-prompt protocols (pair c=2: 17.08 and 14.17 against 20.28) because the speculative route's TTFT is 3.4x the AR arm's (11.9-13.0 s against 3.5 s on ~840-token prompts): provider activation is paid during prefill and returns nothing when the group is refused. That cost is independent of grouping and is the larger lever on this protocol.",
  "reproducibility": "the AR control was re-run on the split server at both concurrencies: pair c=2 20.28 against 20.29 tok/s end to end (21.72 against 22.01 excluding TTFT) and pair c=4 22.08 against 22.10 (25.68 against 25.52), so the arm comparison is not server-instance drift."
 },
 "decision": {
  "split": "rejected: not promoted; the flag stays default-off so the arm remains reproducible",
  "coupling": "kept: a due group the provider refuses as a whole decodes autoregressively in one batch, which is what the adapter's partition contract already specified",
  "targeted_variant": "not retained either: the split's post-crossing window (the healthy row cycling beside the refused row's AR steps) is exactly the window a targeted split would open, and the healthy row measured 9.0 tok/s in it against 10.9 tok/s when it stayed AR",
  "next_levers": [
   "do not pay provider activation for a row whose due group cannot be served: the refused speculative arms spend 3.4x the AR TTFT and then decode at the AR rate",
   "a wider realized group still needs the width-2 execution path (the 2026-09-18 wider-realized-groups artifact measured it inert) and its own economics; serial one-row cycles are now measured as the worse alternative"
  ]
 },
